\import{fdip-macros}
\meta{agent-authored}{true}
\tag{fdip}
\title{Continuum descriptions from finite cycles}
\date{2026-11-06}

\p{When does a description of continuous space come back from finite
physics, without being put in at the start? Part VII compared a
discrete field with a circle that was supplied. \ref{fdip-004J}
is one such comparison. The precisions below are computed on a
finite cycle. A consistent family of those precisions extends to
a law on the dyadic index, with a Hölder version on the completion
and a comparison of the covariance with a Green function.}

\p{One six-cycle is read first. The precision and the kept sites
are defined next. The retained precision is computed for every
integer factor, and the shapes left unchanged are compared with
the precision of the [circle mesh](fdip-004H). Unequal arcs are
then assembled into that law. Block averages of the circle mesh
are compared with the same covariance. Symbols of finite
range that share the second-order coefficient of that mesh
are compared with it as well. The interpolation of the
circle mesh is compared with the resolvent of
#{-d^2/dx^2+\mu}. Vertices of a directed cycle are
compared with arc length, and logarithmic derivatives of
heat traces are compared on a product of graphs. Squared
increments of the continuous field are compared with arc
length, and the resistance between two vertices of a unit
cycle is computed.}

